For EACH assigned finding, try to build the smallest real scenario that would exercise it, using the actual code (compile with \`cargo build -p callisto-cli\` or \`cargo build --workspace\`, then run the real \`callisto\` binary -- e.g. \`./target/debug/callisto version --format json\` -- against a constructed tmp workspace; or write and run a small targeted \`cargo test\`/\`cargo run --example\` if that's a more direct way to exercise the specific function). Report status:
- "reproduced" if you got the real system to actually exhibit the described gap/bug, with the exact command(s) you ran and the real output as evidence.
- "not_reproducible" if you tried and the real runtime behavior does NOT match what the static finding claims (e.g. it turns out to work correctly, or fails differently) -- explain exactly what you observed instead.
- "not_applicable" for findings that are pure code-facts not really susceptible to a runtime repro (e.g. "this dependency is declared but unused", "this struct is missing a field") -- for these, just note that a static read already settles it and no separate runtime repro adds information; don't force a fake repro.

Prioritize getting through all your assigned findings with a real attempt over spending unlimited time perfecting any one repro -- if something is hard to set up in a few minutes, note what you tried and move on.

Additionally, while you have this part of the system running for real: proactively look for OTHER runtime-only problems you notice that were NOT in the static findings list -- panics, unexpected non-zero/zero exit codes, JSON output that doesn't match what any doc claims, confusing or wrong error messages, hangs, or anything that surprised you. Report these as new_issues with your own severity judgment and category. If your filtered findings list for this crate is empty, skip straight to this open-ended sweep.`
