### F.3 Dependency and I/O posture

> `[SPEC DECISION, not in 00-design.md: `callisto-format`'s public API is filesystem-free —
> every function operates on in-memory `&str`/owned values, never a `Path`.]` §6.1's
> "Filenames arbitrary, sorted for deterministic read order" describes a directory-listing
> concern, not a single-file parse concern; nothing in §6 requires this crate itself to walk
> `.changeset/`. Keeping I/O out is the smallest decision consistent with two things the
> design doc already commits to: the compute/apply split (§0.1 rule 3 — "every command is a
> pure function… plus a separate step that touches disk") and this crate's primitive status,
> which would otherwise force every consumer wanting a different directory-walk strategy
> (in-memory fixtures, a real filesystem, a virtual-path-mapped WASM sandbox) to work around
> a decision this crate has no business making for them.

Concretely: `callisto-graph` reads `.changeset/*.md` files off disk in filename-sorted order
(§G.6.1) and hands each file's *contents* to `parse_changeset`; `callisto-cli` writes the
string `write_changeset` returns (§CLI.6.1). Because this crate performs no I/O, it trivially
satisfies §0.1 rule 2's `wasm32-wasip1` + `wasmtime` CI requirement; it is in that job for
completeness and to catch a future PR accidentally adding I/O, not because it is expected to
be the thing that fails it.

### F.4 Module layout

```
callisto-format/
└── src/
    ├── lib.rs             # crate docs, public re-exports
    ├── changeset/
    │   ├── mod.rs          # Changeset, Entry, ParseError, WriteError,
    │   │                   #   parse_changeset, write_changeset
    │   └── frontmatter.rs  # private: line-level name/severity tokenizer, quoting predicate
    ├── bump.rs             # Versioning, SemVerVersioning, bump_version, BumpError
    └── pre.rs              # PreState, PreMode, PreJsonError, parse_pre_json, write_pre_json
```

```rust
// lib.rs
//! Byte-compatible reader/writer for `@changesets/cli`'s file formats.
//!
//! - The changeset markdown format (§6.1): frontmatter parsing/writing, the quoted-vs-bare
//!   name grammar, the empty-changeset validity rule.
//! - `bump_version` (§6.2) and the `Versioning` trait (§7.7).
//! - `pre.json`'s byte shape (§6.4, §8).
//!
//! Depends only on `callisto-model` among callisto crates, and performs no I/O — see §F.2
//! and §F.3 of the crate spec for why both boundaries are deliberate.

pub mod bump;
pub mod changeset;
pub mod pre;

{
  "id": "SPEC-TRACK3-IDENTITY-HARDENING",
  "purpose": "Harden callisto-graph's project-discovery and identity-resolution layer so that (1) workspace membership globs actually filter discovered projects, (2) ecosystem-prefixed group-member spellings resolve, (3) same-named packages in different ecosystems at different paths are recognized as distinct packages instead of colliding, and (4) a same-ecosystem dependency-name miss is never silently satisfied by an unrelated package in a different ecosystem.",
  "scope": "crates/callisto-graph/src/locate/ignore_walk.rs (IgnoreWalkLocator::projects and its membership-filter step), crates/callisto-graph/src/identity.rs (IdentityIndex.prefixed population contract, resolve_native, resolve_native_with_fallback), crates/callisto-graph/src/walk.rs (ManifestWalkResolver::build's package_manifest_decls construction, index.prefixed population, and the edge-extraction call site that currently calls resolve_native_with_fallback). Includes reading Cargo [workspace] members/exclude, npm package.json \"workspaces\", and pnpm-workspace.yaml `packages` to build a membership filter; includes the identity-promotion logic described in docs/01-spec.md §G.4.3 (bare name claimed at two different paths in two different ecosystems promotes both to PackageId::Prefixed; bare name claimed at two different paths in the same ecosystem is GraphError::DuplicatePackage).",
  "non_goals": [
    "Track 3a: whether [[package]]/[[package-set]] `match` compares against a package's name or its workspace-relative path (config/resolve.rs, config/pattern.rs) — a separate, gated product decision (Decision 1 in the remediation plan), not part of this spec.",
    "Any change to how a genuine Case D package (legitimate single-package, dual-ecosystem, one directory) resolves its own identity — DW5's regression-safety boundary: the fix here only changes behavior for genuinely different packages at different paths.",
    "Tracks 1, 2, 5, 9 from the remediation plan (fixed-group version engine, platform-manifest writes, changelog rendering, cascade peer-escalation/rewrite-keys) — downstream consumers of identity resolution, sequenced after this track.",
    "Changing PackageId::matches() semantics — .claude/semantic-model/core-identity.md documents these invariants as fixed; all fixes in this spec operate at call sites (ignore_walk.rs, walk.rs, identity.rs resolution logic) never inside PackageId itself.",
    "Yarn Berry's alternate `workspaces` declaration shapes beyond a plain array of glob strings (e.g. `{\"packages\": [...]}` object form) — only the array-of-globs npm/yarn classic form and pnpm-workspace.yaml's `packages:` list are in scope. An object-shaped `workspaces` field is treated as absent (no membership filter) rather than parsed.",
    "Symlinked package directories that resolve outside the workspace root during membership-glob matching — this is already handled by the existing OutsideWorkspaceRoot check in to_workspace_relative and is unchanged by this spec.",
    "Exposing a new public API for the membership-filter result independent of IgnoreWalkLocator::projects() — the filter is an internal step of projects(), not a separately callable function.",
    "Making resolve_native_with_fallback's diagnostic message text stable/versioned for external tooling to parse — the message content may change across patch versions; only the resolution *behavior* (returns None on 0 or 2+ cross-ecosystem candidates by default) is a stable contract."
  ],
  "api_surface": [
    {
      "name": "IgnoreWalkLocator::projects",
      "signature": "impl ProjectLocator for IgnoreWalkLocator { fn projects(&self) -> Result<Vec<ProjectRoot>, LocateError> }",
      "description": "Must apply a workspace-membership filter after manifest discovery and before emitting each ProjectRoot: a candidate (dir, Cargo) survives only if the root's Cargo.toml has no [workspace] table, or dir matches a members glob and does not match an exclude glob; a candidate (dir, Npm) survives only if the root's package.json has no \"workspaces\" field and no sibling pnpm-workspace.yaml exists, or dir matches a workspaces/packages glob. Absent membership declarations mean no filter for that ecosystem (a lone [package]-only Cargo.toml or a plain package.json with no workspaces field admits every candidate of that ecosystem)."
    },
    {
      "name": "IdentityIndex.prefixed",
      "signature": "pub prefixed: BTreeMap<(Ecosystem, String), PackageId>",
      "description": "Must be populated during ManifestWalkResolver::build for every (ecosystem, native-name) pair discovered, mirroring the population of index.native, so that resolve_human's existing lookup logic over self.prefixed (identity.rs lines 267-286) can resolve an ecosystem-prefixed group-member spelling such as cargo:foo or npm:foo to a real, discovered PackageId."
    },
    {
      "name": "ManifestWalkResolver::build (package identity collision keying)",
      "signature": "pub fn build<L: ProjectLocator, R: CommandRunner>(root: &Path, locator: &L, _runner: &R, cfg: &ResolvedConfig, manifest_cache: &RefCell<BTreeMap<PathBuf, Arc<dyn Manifest>>>) -> Result<Self, GraphError>",
      "description": "The by-path grouping and per-path primary_id selection (Case D collapse) are already correct and unchanged. The package_manifest_decls.insert collision point must, before erroring, distinguish 'same bare name claimed at two different paths in two different ecosystems' (promote both occurrences' PackageId to PackageId::Prefixed{ecosystem, name} per docs/01-spec.md §G.4.3, no error) from 'same bare name claimed at two different paths in the same ecosystem' (GraphError::DuplicatePackage{id, paths}, unchanged error behavior) and from 'same path, multiple ecosystems' (already collapsed upstream into one Package, never reaches this collision point)."
    },
    {
      "name": "IdentityIndex::resolve_native",
      "signature": "pub fn resolve_native(&self, eco: Ecosystem, name: &str) -> Option<&PackageId>",
      "description": "Unchanged signature and unchanged same-ecosystem-first, cross-ecosystem-fallback-on-exactly-one-candidate behavior. Retained as the permissive lookup for callers (e.g. group-member resolution) that intentionally want cross-ecosystem fallback."
    },
    {
      "name": "IdentityIndex::resolve_native_with_fallback",
      "signature": "pub fn resolve_native_with_fallback<'a>(&'a self, eco: Ecosystem, name: &str, diagnostics: &mut Vec<callisto_model::Diagnostic>) -> Option<&'a PackageId>",
      "description": "The dependency-edge-extraction call site in walk.rs (currently calling this function at the point matching docs/01-spec.md §G.4.4's edge-extraction step) must stop resolving a same-ecosystem lookup miss to a cross-ecosystem name match by default. When the same-ecosystem lookup misses, the function returns None (the dependency is treated as external/unresolved) rather than falling back to a cross-ecosystem candidate, matching docs/01-spec.md §G.4.4's stated intent ('a Cargo serde dependency must not resolve to an npm workspace member called serde'). When exactly one cross-ecosystem candidate exists and would previously have been silently returned, a Diagnostic (severity Warning, code UnknownPackage) is still pushed naming the ambiguity and suggesting the ecosystem-prefixed spelling, but the function itself now returns None in that case too — the diagnostic becomes advisory-only, not a description of a resolution that happened."
    }
  ],
  "acceptance_criteria": [
    {
      "id": "AC-01",
      "criterion": "Given a workspace root Cargo.toml with [workspace] members = [\"crates/*\"] and exclude = [\"crates/scratch-example\"], and crates/scratch-example/Cargo.toml containing a valid [package] table, calling IgnoreWalkLocator::new(root).projects() returns a Vec<ProjectRoot> that contains no entry with path == \"crates/scratch-example\".",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-02",
      "criterion": "Given the same workspace as AC-01, and crates/kept-example/Cargo.toml (matching the members glob, not excluded) containing a valid [package] table, calling projects() returns exactly one entry with path == \"crates/kept-example\" and ecosystem == Ecosystem::Cargo.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-03",
      "criterion": "Given a root package.json declaring {\"workspaces\": [\"packages/*\"]}, and a package.json at a path outside every workspaces glob — e.g. tools/helper/package.json with a valid \"name\" field — calling projects() returns no entry with path == \"tools/helper\".",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-04",
      "criterion": "Given a root with no package.json \"workspaces\" field but a sibling pnpm-workspace.yaml containing `packages:\\n  - \"packages/*\"`, a package.json at packages/kept/package.json (matching the glob) is discovered by projects(), and a package.json at tools/outside/package.json (not matching the glob) is not discovered by projects().",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-05",
      "criterion": "Given a Cargo.toml at the workspace root with no [workspace] table at all (a single-crate repo), every Cargo candidate directory discovered by the walk is included in projects() — the absence of a [workspace] table means no membership filter applies, not that zero packages are admitted.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-06",
      "criterion": "Given a root package.json with no \"workspaces\" field and no pnpm-workspace.yaml anywhere in the workspace, every Npm candidate directory discovered by the walk is included in projects() — absence of both markers means no npm membership filter applies.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-07",
      "criterion": "Given a real discovered Cargo package named foo (project root path 'crates/foo', ecosystem Cargo) and a callisto.toml with `[[fixed-group]]\\nname = \"g\"\\nmembers = [\"cargo:foo\"]`, loading the workspace via Workspace::load succeeds and the resolved fixed group's members contain the PackageId that IdentityIndex resolved for the Cargo package foo; no GraphError::MissingGroupMember is raised for that member.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-08",
      "criterion": "For the workspace in AC-07, IdentityIndex.prefixed contains an entry keyed (Ecosystem::Cargo, \"foo\".to_string()) whose value equals the PackageId that IdentityIndex.native.get(&(Ecosystem::Cargo, \"foo\".to_string())) also returns for that same package — the same identity is reachable through both the native map and the prefixed map for every discovered (ecosystem, native-name) pair.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-09",
      "criterion": "Given the same workspace as AC-07 but with members = [\"npm:foo\"] where no npm package named foo exists anywhere in the workspace (only the Cargo package foo exists), loading the workspace via Workspace::load returns Err(GraphError::MissingGroupMember { group, member }) with member == \"npm:foo\" — an ecosystem-prefixed spelling for an ecosystem in which the name does not exist must still fail, not silently match the wrong ecosystem's foo.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-10",
      "criterion": "Given a Cargo crate at crates/native-core (Cargo.toml package.name = \"native-core\") and an unrelated npm package at packages/native-core (package.json name = \"native-core\"), with no shared directory and no callisto.toml config referencing either by ecosystem-prefixed spelling, building the workspace via ManifestWalkResolver::build succeeds (Ok, not Err(GraphError::DuplicatePackage)), and the resulting packages map contains exactly two entries: one with id == PackageId::Prefixed { ecosystem: Ecosystem::Cargo, name: \"native-core\".to_string() } and one with id == PackageId::Prefixed { ecosystem: Ecosystem::Npm, name: \"native-core\".to_string() }.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-11",
      "criterion": "For the workspace in AC-10, IdentityIndex.native contains both (Ecosystem::Cargo, \"native-core\") mapping to the Cargo package's PackageId and (Ecosystem::Npm, \"native-core\") mapping to the distinct Npm package's PackageId — the two identities are never coalesced into one entry.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-12",
      "criterion": "Given the standard maturin/PyO3 layout — a Rust crate at bindings/rust (Cargo.toml package.name = \"mylib\") and its Python binding at bindings/python (pyproject.toml project.name = \"mylib\"), sibling directories, no shared path — building the workspace succeeds without GraphError::DuplicatePackage, producing two distinct packages with id == PackageId::Prefixed{Cargo, \"mylib\"} and id == PackageId::Prefixed{Pypi, \"mylib\"} respectively.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-13",
      "criterion": "Given a Cargo crate at crates/dup-a (name = \"dup\") and a second, unrelated Cargo crate at crates/dup-b (name = \"dup\") — same ecosystem, two different paths — building the workspace returns Err(GraphError::DuplicatePackage { id, paths }) where paths contains both \"crates/dup-a\" and \"crates/dup-b\" in some order, matching the pre-existing, unchanged same-ecosystem collision behavior.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-14",
      "criterion": "Given a genuine Case D package at one directory (Cargo.toml name = \"foo\", package.json name = \"@myorg/foo\", both at path 'crates/foo', neither name claimed by any other package anywhere in the workspace), building the workspace succeeds and produces exactly one Package with id == PackageId::Bare(\"foo\".to_string()) (unchanged from current behavior: the primary-ecosystem-priority selection at the same path is untouched by the DW4 fix, since same-path collapse happens before the cross-path collision check).",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-15",
      "criterion": "Given a Cargo package foo at crates/foo and no npm package named foo anywhere in the workspace, and a separate npm package consumer at packages/consumer whose package.json declares \"dependencies\": {\"foo\": \"^1.0.0\"}, building the workspace's dependency edges does not resolve consumer's \"foo\" dependency to the Cargo package foo's PackageId — ws.graph.edges() contains no DepEdge with to == the Cargo foo's PackageId and from == consumer's PackageId; the dependency is treated as unresolved/external and produces no edge for that entry.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-16",
      "criterion": "For the scenario in AC-15, ws.diagnostics (or the diagnostics collected during ManifestWalkResolver::build) contains a Diagnostic with code == DiagnosticCode::UnknownPackage, severity == DiagnosticSeverity::Warning, and message containing the string \"foo\" and the word \"ambiguous\" or \"cargo:foo\" — the miss is surfaced, not silent, even though no edge is created.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-17",
      "criterion": "Given an npm package consumer whose package.json declares a dependency \"serde\": \"^1\" in a workspace containing a Cargo crate named serde (at any path) and no npm package named serde anywhere, calling IdentityIndex::resolve_native(Ecosystem::Npm, \"serde\") directly returns Some for the cross-ecosystem match (resolve_native's own contract is unchanged — it still performs cross-ecosystem fallback on exactly one candidate), but the actual edge-extraction call site in ManifestWalkResolver::build (which now calls resolve_native_with_fallback with the corrected no-fallback-on-miss behavior, or an equivalent non-fallback path) produces no DepEdge from consumer to the Cargo serde package — reproducing docs/01-spec.md §G.4.4's worked example exactly.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-18",
      "criterion": "Given two cross-ecosystem candidates both named ambiguous-lib (a Cargo package and a Pypi package, both distinct from the requesting package's own ecosystem, e.g. an npm consumer depending on \"ambiguous-lib\" where both a Cargo and a Pypi package share that bare name and neither is an npm package), the edge-extraction call site resolves to None (no edge created) and diagnostics contains exactly one Diagnostic with code == DiagnosticCode::UnknownPackage listing both candidate ecosystem-prefixed spellings in its message (e.g. containing both \"cargo:ambiguous-lib\" and \"pypi:ambiguous-lib\") — this is the true-ambiguity (2+ candidates) case, already partially handled by the existing resolve_native_with_fallback diagnostic branch, and must continue to produce exactly one diagnostic, not zero and not two.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-19",
      "criterion": "Given a workspace root Cargo.toml with a syntactically malformed [workspace] members value (members = \"crates/*\" — a bare string instead of an array, which toml_edit parses successfully as a document but fails schema expectations for a glob-list field), IgnoreWalkLocator::projects() does not panic and does not silently admit every candidate as if no membership filter existed; it treats the malformed members field as absent (no filter applied, matching AC-05's absent-declaration behavior) rather than erroring the entire walk, since a malformed root config must not make the workspace entirely undiscoverable.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-20",
      "criterion": "Given a root package.json with \"workspaces\": [] (present but empty array) and at least one package.json elsewhere in the tree, IgnoreWalkLocator::projects() returns zero Npm-ecosystem entries — an explicitly empty workspaces list is a real membership filter (matches nothing) and is distinguished from an absent \"workspaces\" field (AC-06), not conflated with it.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-21",
      "criterion": "Given a pnpm-workspace.yaml that is present but fails to parse as valid YAML (malformed syntax), IgnoreWalkLocator::projects() does not panic and does not error the entire walk; it treats the npm membership filter as absent for that workspace (falls back to AC-06's no-filter behavior), same treatment as AC-19's malformed-TOML case.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-22",
      "criterion": "Each of DW1 (AC-01/AC-02), DW2 (AC-03/AC-04), DW3 (AC-07/AC-08), DW4 (AC-10 through AC-14), and DW6 (AC-15 through AC-18) ships with at least one regression test whose test-body setup reproduces the exact scenario shape described in the requirement's 'why' section (a scratch-example crate excluded via Cargo workspace exclude; IdentityIndex.prefixed grep-verified populated; the maturin/PyO3 sibling-directory same-name-different-ecosystem layout; and the cross-ecosystem serde worked example), not only a synthetic minimal-case test with arbitrary names.",
      "is_error_case": false
    }
  ],
  "linked_requirement": "REQ-TRACK3-IDENTITY-HARDENING",
  "reasoning": "Grounding: read ignore_walk.rs (confirms zero membership-filter logic exists), identity.rs (confirms prefixed field exists but is never written by walk.rs, and resolve_native_with_fallback's cross-ecosystem fallback-on-exactly-one-candidate is real and reachable), walk.rs (confirms package_manifest_decls.insert keys purely on primary_id computed from by-path grouping; same-path Case D collapse is correct per spec §G.4.3's first rule, but the cross-path cross-ecosystem promotion-to-Prefixed described in §G.4.3's second rule is entirely unimplemented), docs/01-spec.md §G.2.2 (confirms membership filter is a real spec requirement) and §G.4.2/§G.4.3/§G.4.4 (confirms the promotion algorithm and that the documented edge-extraction pseudocode calls resolve_native, not resolve_native_with_fallback, at the edge-extraction site). Underspecified items flagged: (1) DW4's exact disambiguation mechanism is not stated in the requirement — resolved using §G.4.3's own documented promotion algorithm since it is already the spec's stated design; (2) whether resolve_native_with_fallback should be deleted vs. behaviorally changed is not stated — resolved as 'keep the function, change its default no-match behavior to return None on both 0 and 2+ candidates, keep the diagnostic for the 2+ case as advisory', since resolve_native itself stays useful to other callers (AC-17) and this is the most spec-conformant reading, but it is a judgment call the implementer should confirm at plan time; (3) malformed-glob and malformed-YAML behavior (AC-19/AC-21) is not specified anywhere in the requirement or §G.2.2 — chose 'treat as absent filter, do not error the whole walk' as the safer default consistent with §G.2.2's own text, flagged here as a genuine spec-writer judgment call rather than silently assumed; (4) AC-20 (empty workspaces array) is a plausible but unstated edge case added because it is the natural nil/malformed/out-of-range boundary for DW2's glob-matching logic, per the parent task's explicit instruction to cover such boundaries."
}

