      "description": "Must apply a workspace-membership filter after manifest discovery and before emitting each ProjectRoot. Cargo rule: a candidate (dir, Cargo) survives only if (a) the root Cargo.toml has no [workspace] table, or does not exist at all as a file (both cases mean no Cargo membership filter applies, identical treatment), or (b) dir matches a members glob and does not match an exclude glob, or (c) the root Cargo.toml declares both a [package] table and a [workspace] table and dir is the workspace root itself — Cargo treats the root's own [package] as an implicit workspace member even though it appears in no members glob entry, so it must never be silently dropped (see AC-07), or (d) the root Cargo.toml has a [workspace] table with no members key at all and dir does not match an exclude glob. A [workspace] table present with no members key at all is treated identically to an absent [workspace] table for filtering purposes — every Cargo candidate that does not match a well-formed exclude glob is admitted, matching AC-05's behavior (the exclude list, when present, is still honored — see AC-15), not as an empty members = [] list (which excludes everything per AC-10c). A malformed exclude value (present but not an array of strings) is treated as an absent exclude list alone — nothing is excluded — while a well-formed members filter, if present, still applies normally; a malformed exclude value never causes every candidate to be admitted as if members were also absent (see AC-09b). Npm/pnpm rule and precedence: when a root has a pnpm-workspace.yaml, its packages: list governs npm-ecosystem membership filtering and takes precedence over any package.json \"workspaces\" field present at the same root — the \"workspaces\" field is consulted only when no pnpm-workspace.yaml exists at that root (see AC-08; rationale: a pnpm-managed repo may retain a stale \"workspaces\" field for npm/yarn-tooling or IDE compatibility, and pnpm-workspace.yaml is the source of truth for a pnpm-managed tree). A candidate (dir, Npm) survives only if (a) no \"workspaces\" field and no pnpm-workspace.yaml exist at the root (no filter, every candidate admitted), or (b) a pnpm-workspace.yaml exists, parses successfully, and has a packages: value that is a YAML sequence of strings, and dir matches one of its glob entries, or (c) no such parseable-and-valid-shape pnpm-workspace.yaml exists (whether because the file is absent, fails to parse, or lacks a valid packages: sequence), a \"workspaces\" field exists, and dir matches one of its glob entries. A pnpm-workspace.yaml that fails to parse (AC-11), that parses but has a missing or non-sequence-of-strings packages: value (AC-11b), or that parses successfully to zero YAML documents (AC-11d — an empty, whitespace-only, or comments-only file, for which yaml_rust2::YamlLoader::load_from_str returns Ok(vec![]) rather than Err or a single document) does not count as \"present\" for this precedence rule — it is treated identically to the pnpm-workspace.yaml being entirely absent, so a package.json \"workspaces\" field at the same root is still consulted in that case (see AC-11c). Absent membership declarations mean no filter for that ecosystem; an explicitly empty declaration (\"workspaces\": [] or an empty pnpm packages: list) is a real filter that matches nothing, distinct from absence (see AC-10). pnpm-workspace.yaml's packages: list is parsed using yaml_rust2::YamlLoader::load_from_str (see scope for the new dependency). A pnpm-workspace.yaml for which yaml_rust2::YamlLoader::load_from_str returns Err(..), a pnpm-workspace.yaml that parses successfully but whose packages: value is missing or is not a YAML sequence of strings, a pnpm-workspace.yaml that parses successfully but produces zero YAML documents (Ok(vec![]) — an empty, whitespace-only, or comments-only file; see AC-11d), a Cargo members field that toml_edit parses successfully as a document but that does not deserialize as an array of strings,