      "description": "Members/exclude and npm/pnpm glob patterns are matched using globset::GlobBuilder — confirmed already a dependency of this crate (verified via `grep globset Cargo.toml crates/callisto-graph/Cargo.toml` at the workspace root: workspace root Cargo.toml pins globset = \"0.4\" and crates/callisto-graph/Cargo.toml consumes it via globset = { workspace = true }; no new dependency is added or needed for glob matching, unlike yaml-rust2) against the workspace-relative directory path, with literal_separator(true): `*` matches exactly one path segment and does not cross `/`; `**` matches zero or more full path segments recursively — mirroring the semantics of the `glob` crate Cargo itself uses for [workspace] members/exclude. Matching is case-sensitive against forward-slash-normalized workspace-relative paths. Per-entry glob-compile-failure rule (general principle governing every ecosystem's membership-declaration lists): when globset::GlobBuilder::build() (or equivalent Glob compilation) fails for one glob string within an otherwise well-formed list of glob strings — a Cargo members or exclude entry, an npm \"workspaces\" entry, or a pnpm packages: entry — only that single entry is treated as never-matching (an entry that admits nothing); every other syntactically valid entry in the same list is still matched normally, and neither a whole-list fallback-to-absent, nor a panic, nor a whole-walk error occurs. This is the narrowest-possible-scope-of-degradation rule, consistent with AC-09b (a malformed exclude value doesn't disable the members filter), AC-09c (a malformed workspaces field doesn't affect anything else at that root), and AC-11b (a malformed packages key doesn't kill YAML parsing of the rest of the document); it applies to every per-entry glob-parse failure across every ecosystem's lists in this spec (see AC-09f for Cargo members, AC-09i for Cargo exclude, AC-09g for npm workspaces, AC-09h for pnpm packages). (verified against globset::GlobBuilder::build()'s Result<Glob, Error> signature. Note: this is a deliberate divergence from tags.rs's matching_tags(), which treats a malformed TagTemplate glob as Err propagated to the caller — see tags.rs's rationale (lines 38-42, and the confirming test test_last_tag_for_propagates_malformed_glob_error at line 409) that a malformed tag template must never silently pick an unrelated package's tag. That whole-operation-fails posture is appropriate for tag resolution, where a wrong tag is a correctness hazard; membership filtering instead uses narrowest-scope-skip per this spec's owner because one malformed entry in a members/exclude/workspaces/packages list should not make the entire workspace undiscoverable.)"
