  "linked_requirement": "REQ-TRACK3-IDENTITY-HARDENING-A",
  "revision_note": "Tenth correction round, applying all 4 blockers from the first canon-exit-gate submission (round 9's canon-auditor pass was clean, but this is the first exit-gate run against SPEC-TRACK3A-WORKSPACE-MEMBERSHIP specifically): (1) AC-09 was self-contradictory — its first clause (\"does not silently admit every candidate as if no membership filter existed\") demanded exactly the outcome its second clause (\"treats the malformed members field as absent... matching AC-05's absent-declaration behavior\") mandated, since AC-05's absent-declaration behavior is precisely admit-every-candidate. Both api_surface entry 1 and AC-14 already took the fall-back-to-absent (admit-all) reading, so that is the settled direction; deleted the contradicting first clause from AC-09's criterion text, keeping only the fall-back-to-absent/no-panic/no-whole-walk-error requirement. (2) Three-way inconsistency for a [workspace] table with exclude present but no members key: api_surface entry 1's enumerated Cargo survival rule ((a)/(b)/(c)) had no arm matching this shape and so implied every such candidate is dropped, while the entry's own supplementary sentence and api_surface entry 3 both said unqualified \"every Cargo candidate is admitted\" (dropping nothing, ignoring exclude), and AC-15 requires exclude to be honored in exactly this shape. Added arm (d) to entry 1's enumerated rule (\"the root Cargo.toml has a [workspace] table with no members key at all and dir does not match an exclude glob\"), and qualified both the entry 1 supplementary sentence and the whole of entry 3 to read \"every Cargo candidate that does not match a well-formed exclude glob is admitted... (the exclude list, when present, is still honored — see AC-15)\" in place of the unqualified admit-all phrasing. (3) AC-09f's given-clause (\"both crates/kept-example/Cargo.toml and a hypothetical crates/[unterminated-shaped path not existing in the fixture\") read literally as asserting both paths are absent, making the criterion's own assertion that projects() includes crates/kept-example unsatisfiable. Rewrote the given-clause to match AC-14's already-correct restatement of the same fixture: crates/kept-example/Cargo.toml actually exists with a valid [package] table, and no directory matching the literal path crates/[unterminated exists anywhere in the fixture (which is fine — an invalid glob string doesn't need a matching directory to exist). (4) AC-14 named candidate-less fixtures for 8 error criteria (AC-09, AC-09b, AC-09c, AC-09d, AC-09e, AC-11, AC-11b, AC-11d), so those regression tests would pass vacuously (empty projects() result under every competing interpretation, proving only no-panic). Extended each of the 8 fixture descriptions in AC-14 with at least one concrete candidate manifest and its expected outcome, following the pattern already used correctly for AC-09f/g/h/i: AC-09 and AC-09c each gained an out-of-glob candidate (tools/outside/Cargo.toml, tools/outside/package.json) at a path that would NOT match if the malformed field were misread as a literal single-entry glob, disambiguating true fall-back-to-absent from that misreading; AC-09b gained the full three-manifest fixture (crates/scratch-example, crates/kept-example, tools/outside) proving both halves of its claim (malformed exclude excludes nothing; well-formed members still applies); AC-09d, AC-09e, AC-11, AC-11b, and AC-11d each gained one elsewhere-in-tree candidate manifest (crates/kept/Cargo.toml or packages/kept/package.json) proving the walk continues and admits candidates rather than merely not panicking. No other criterion, non_goals entry, or api_surface text was touched. Prior ninth-round revision_note preserved below for history: Ninth correction round, applying the single canon-auditor finding from round 8 (empirically verified: the auditor built and ran a throwaway crate to confirm yaml_rust2::YamlLoader::load_from_str's actual behavior on empty/whitespace/comments-only input): AC-11 covers pnpm-workspace.yaml content that fails to parse (Err), and AC-11b covers content that parses successfully but has a missing or wrong-shaped packages: key — but a third, distinct code path was uncovered: an empty, whitespace-only, or comments-only pnpm-workspace.yaml causes yaml_rust2::YamlLoader::load_from_str to return Ok(vec![]) — a successful parse producing zero YAML documents, neither an Err nor a single (wrong-shaped) document. The idiomatic docs[0] or docs.first().unwrap() access pattern would panic on this input, and neither AC-11 nor AC-11b's fixtures exercise it. Added AC-11d (is_error_case: true, matching AC-11/AC-11b's classification): given a pnpm-workspace.yaml that is empty, whitespace-only, or comments-only such that load_from_str returns Ok(vec![]), IgnoreWalkLocator::projects() does not panic and treats the npm membership filter as absent, the same fallback as AC-11b's missing-packages-key case. Extended the primary api_surface entry's two AC-11/AC-11b cross-reference sentences (the precedence-rule sentence and the never-panic/never-abort sentence) to name AC-11d alongside them, and added an explicit note that the implementation must never unconditionally index docs[0] or call docs.first().unwrap() on the Vec<Yaml> returned by YamlLoader::load_from_str. Extended AC-14's criterion group list and regression-fixture text to include AC-11d with the exact fixture: a pnpm-workspace.yaml containing only \"# no packages here\\n\". No other criterion, non_goals entry, or reasoning text was touched. Prior eighth-round revision_note preserved below for history: Eighth correction round, applying both canon-auditor findings from round 7: (1) missing-error-case — the glob-matching-semantics api_surface entry claimed the per-entry glob-compile-failure rule applies to Cargo members, Cargo exclude, npm workspaces, and pnpm packages, but only 3 of 4 (AC-09f/g/h) had a proving fixture; Cargo's exclude list was asserted by prose only. Added AC-09i (is_error_case: true): workspace root Cargo.toml with members = [\"crates/*\"] and exclude = [\"crates/scratch-example\", \"crates/[unterminated\"], with crates/scratch-example/Cargo.toml and crates/kept-example/Cargo.toml both valid — crates/scratch-example is excluded (well-formed entry honored), crates/kept-example is included, and the invalid exclude entry is treated as never-matching without panicking or erroring the whole walk, mirroring AC-09f for Cargo's exclude list specifically. Extended AC-14's criterion group and regression-fixture text to name AC-09i alongside AC-09f/AC-09g/AC-09h. Updated the glob-matching-semantics api_surface entry's AC-09f-only citation to \"(see AC-09f for Cargo members, AC-09i for Cargo exclude, AC-09g for npm workspaces, AC-09h for pnpm packages)\". (2) incomplete — pnpm-workspace.yaml's packages: list supports a real, documented '!'-prefixed negation syntax that this spec never addressed, unlike every other out-of-scope input shape (Yarn Berry object-form, Python monorepo mechanisms), which each received an explicit non_goals carve-out. Added a non_goals entry stating that a '!'-prefixed packages: entry is passed to globset::GlobBuilder like any other entry, that no real workspace-relative path begins with the literal '!' character so such an entry always fails to match and is functionally inert, and that pnpm's negation semantics are therefore not honored by IgnoreWalkLocator::projects() after this spec lands — support for pnpm's negation-prefix syntax is out of scope and tracked as a follow-up. No other criterion, non_goals entry, or reasoning text was touched. Prior seventh-round revision_note preserved below for history: Seventh correction round, applying the single canon-auditor finding from round 6 (1 substantive issue, 2 findings sharing one root cause): AC-08 establishes pnpm-workspace.yaml takes precedence over package.json's \"workspaces\" field whenever both are present at the same root, but AC-11/AC-11b's \"treated as absent\" fallback for a malformed or wrong-shape pnpm-workspace.yaml was never reconciled with AC-08 — it was ambiguous whether \"absent\" meant only the pnpm marker itself is treated as absent (so package.json's workspaces field is then consulted per AC-08's fallback) or the entire npm filter becomes absent (ignoring a present, valid package.json workspaces field too). No fixture tested a root with both a broken pnpm-workspace.yaml and a valid package.json workspaces field. Resolved in favor of the first reading: rewrote AC-11's criterion text to scope its fixture explicitly to no-package.json-workspaces-field-present and to point to the new AC-11c for the interaction case. Added AC-11c (is_error_case: true, matching AC-11/AC-11b's classification): root package.json {\"workspaces\": [\"packages/*\"]} plus a malformed pnpm-workspace.yaml at the same root — packages/y/package.json is still discovered via the package.json workspaces field, because a malformed (or, per AC-11b, wrong-shape) pnpm-workspace.yaml does not count as \"present\" for AC-08's precedence purposes; only a pnpm-workspace.yaml that exists, parses successfully, and has a valid packages: sequence of strings suppresses package.json's workspaces field. Tightened the Npm rule in the primary api_surface entry's clauses (b) and (c) to state this precedence condition precisely — (b) now requires the pnpm-workspace.yaml to parse successfully and have a packages: value that is a YAML sequence of strings, not merely to exist; (c) now applies whenever no such parseable-and-valid-shape pnpm-workspace.yaml exists, whether absent, unparseable, or wrong-shaped — and added an explicit cross-reference to AC-11/AC-11b/AC-11c. Extended AC-13's regression-fixture group and criterion list to include AC-11c with its exact fixture. No other criterion, non_goals entry, or reasoning text was touched. Prior sixth-round revision_note preserved below for history: Sixth correction round, applying the single canon-auditor finding from round 5: this spec's existing non_goals[11] (the track-g-matrix-napi-maturin.json AC-010/AC-010b line-shift flag) correctly identified that this spec's membership-filter insertion into IgnoreWalkLocator::projects() will shift line numbers in crates/callisto-graph/src/locate/ignore_walk.rs, but a second spec pinning exact line citations into that same file was never flagged: .claude/specs/SPEC-APPLY-BATCH-002.json's AC-015 and its revision-2 reasoning cite crates/callisto-graph/src/locate/ignore_walk.rs:145-161 (the to_workspace_relative function) with lines 152-154 called out specifically for the workspace-root '.' case, as part of reproducing that function's behavior for a cross-file PathBuf-equality proof. Read SPEC-APPLY-BATCH-002.json directly and confirmed these exact line numbers and the exact citation text (ignore_walk.rs:145-161 for to_workspace_relative; 152-154 for the workspace-root case) before adding a twelfth non_goals entry, parallel in structure and wording pattern to the existing eleventh entry, naming SPEC-APPLY-BATCH-002.json's citations and requiring the same post-landing re-verification-and-update follow-up already required for track-g's AC-010/AC-010b. No other criterion, non_goals entry, api_surface entry, or reasoning text was touched. Prior fifth-round revision_note preserved below for history: Fifth correction round, applying the single canon-auditor finding from round 4 of the post-split audit cycle: the glob-matching-semantics api_surface entry states the narrowest-scope-skip-on-glob-compile-failure rule applies to a Cargo members/exclude entry, an npm \"workspaces\" entry, or a pnpm packages: entry, but AC-09f — the only fixture-backed proof of this rule — tests only the Cargo case; no criterion exercised npm or pnpm with one malformed glob entry among well-formed ones. Added AC-09g (root package.json {\"workspaces\": [\"packages/kept-example\", \"packages/[unterminated\"]}, packages/kept-example/package.json with a valid \"name\" field; pac